{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "def search(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the element that appears only once in a sorted array where all other elements appear twice.\n    Assumes the input array is sorted and contains exactly one unique element.\n    \"\"\"\n    low = 0\n    high = len(arr) - 1\n\n    while low < high:\n        mid = low + (high - low) // 2\n\n        # If mid is even, check arr[mid] with arr[mid + 1]\n        # If mid is odd, check arr[mid] with arr[mid - 1]\n        if mid % 2 == 0:\n            if arr[mid] == arr[mid + 1]:\n                # The unique element is to the right\n                low = mid + 2\n            else:\n                # The unique element is at or to the left of mid\n                high = mid\n        else: # mid is odd\n            if arr[mid] == arr[mid - 1]:\n                # The unique element is to the right\n                low = mid + 1\n            else:\n                # The unique element is at or to the left of mid - 1\n                high = mid - 1\n\n    return arr[low]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/120", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple\ndef max_product_tuple(pairs: List[Tuple[int, int]]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum absolute product between numbers in pairs of tuples within a given list.\n    It iterates through each tuple, calculates the absolute product of its elements, and tracks the maximum found.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_absolute_product_found = 0\n\n    for num1, num2 in pairs:\n        current_product = num1 * num2\n        current_abs_product = abs(current_product)\n        if current_abs_product > max_absolute_product_found:\n            max_absolute_product_found = current_abs_product\n            \n    return max_absolute_product_found"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/123", "solution": "def amicable_numbers_sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of all amicable numbers up to a specified number n.\n    An amicable pair (a, b) consists of two different numbers such that the sum of the proper divisors of a is b,\n    and the sum of the proper divisors of b is a.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 220:\n        return 0\n\n    # proper_divisors_sum[i] will store the sum of proper divisors for i.\n    proper_divisors_sum = [0] * (n + 1)\n\n    # Compute sum of proper divisors for all numbers up to n using a sieve-like approach.\n    for i in range(1, n // 2 + 1):\n        for j in range(2 * i, n + 1, i):\n            proper_divisors_sum[j] += i\n\n    total_amicable_sum = 0\n    # Iterate through numbers to find amicable pairs and sum them.\n    for a in range(1, n + 1):\n        b = proper_divisors_sum[a]\n\n        # Check if (a, b) is an amicable pair and b is within the limit,\n        # ensuring each pair is counted once by checking b > a.\n        if b > a and b <= n and proper_divisors_sum[b] == a:\n            total_amicable_sum += a + b\n\n    return total_amicable_sum"}
