{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "import math\ndef max_sum(array: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence for the given array.\n    A sequence is bitonic if it is first increasing and then decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(array)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return array[0]\n\n    # inc[i] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at index i\n    inc = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        inc[i] = array[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            if array[j] < array[i]:\n                inc[i] = max(inc[i], inc[j] + array[i])\n\n    # dec[i] stores the maximum sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at index i\n    dec = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        dec[i] = array[i]\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if array[j] < array[i]:\n                dec[i] = max(dec[i], dec[j] + array[i])\n\n    max_bitonic_sum = -math.inf\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A bitonic subsequence must have at least one increasing and one decreasing part.\n        # This implies that both inc[i] and dec[i] must be greater than array[i] for a valid peak.\n        # However, a sequence that is strictly increasing or strictly decreasing can also be considered bitonic\n        # (with a degenerate decreasing/increasing part). The current logic (inc[i] + dec[i] - array[i])\n        # correctly handles these cases if inc/dec are properly calculated.\n        # For a single element, inc[i] = array[i] and dec[i] = array[i], sum = array[i].\n        # For strictly increasing sequence, dec[i] would just be array[i] for all i.\n        # For strictly decreasing sequence, inc[i] would just be array[i] for all i.\n        # So we should be able to simply take the max over all valid (inc[i] + dec[i] - array[i]) values.\n        max_bitonic_sum = max(max_bitonic_sum, inc[i] + dec[i] - array[i])\n    \n    # If all elements are negative, or if the array is empty, max_bitonic_sum could remain -inf.\n    # The requirement specifies handling negative numbers, and for empty arrays it should return 0.\n    # For an array like [-1, -2, -3], the logic would yield something like -1, -2, -3.\n    # max_bitonic_sum will be at least the largest single element sum if the array is not empty.\n    # Since n > 0 is already handled above, max_bitonic_sum will be initialized to an actual value.\n    \n    return max_bitonic_sum"}
