{"task_id": "Mbpp/256", "solution": "import math\ndef count_Primes_nums(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of prime numbers less than the given non-negative number n.\n    This function implements the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 2:\n        return 0\n\n    # Initialize a boolean array 'is_prime' where is_prime[i] is true if i is potentially prime.\n    is_prime = [True] * n\n    is_prime[0] = False  # 0 is not a prime number\n    is_prime[1] = False  # 1 is not a prime number\n\n    # Iterate from p = 2 up to the square root of n.\n    # We only need to check for prime factors up to sqrt(n) to mark composites.\n    for p in range(2, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1):\n        # If is_prime[p] is still true, then p is a prime number.\n        if is_prime[p]:\n            # Mark all multiples of p (starting from p*p) as not prime.\n            # Multiples smaller than p*p would have already been marked by smaller prime factors.\n            for multiple in range(p * p, n, p):\n                is_prime[multiple] = False\n\n    # Count all numbers that are marked as prime.\n    prime_count = 0\n    for i in range(2, n):\n        if is_prime[i]:\n            prime_count += 1\n\n    return prime_count"}
