{"task_id": "Mbpp/418", "solution": "def Find_Max(sequences):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the element of a list having maximum length.\n    Returns None if the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not sequences:\n        return None\n\n    max_length_sequence = None\n    max_length = -1\n\n    for seq in sequences:\n        current_length = len(seq)\n        if current_length > max_length:\n            max_length = current_length\n            max_length_sequence = seq\n            \n    return max_length_sequence"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "from typing import List, Optional, Tuple\ndef max_Product(arr: List[int]) -> Optional[Tuple[int, int]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return the pair of distinct integers from `arr` that yields the maximum product.\n    If `arr` contains fewer than two elements, return None.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(arr) < 2:\n        return None\n\n    # Track the two largest and two smallest values\n    max1 = max2 = float('-inf')\n    min1 = min2 = float('inf')\n    for num in arr:\n        if num > max1:\n            max2, max1 = max1, num\n        elif num > max2:\n            max2 = num\n\n        if num < min1:\n            min2, min1 = min1, num\n        elif num < min2:\n            min2 = num\n\n    # Compute candidate products\n    prod_max = max1 * max2\n    prod_min = min1 * min2\n\n    # Determine which pair yields the maximum product\n    if prod_max >= prod_min:\n        # Find the first occurrence of max1 and max2\n        first = second = None\n        for val in arr: # Iterating over values, not indices, as per design's example snippet\n            if val == max1 and first is None:\n                first = val\n            elif val == max2 and second is None: # Use elif to ensure distinct placements for values\n                second = val\n            if first is not None and second is not None:\n                break\n        return (first, second)\n    else:\n        # Find the first occurrence of min1 and min2\n        first = second = None\n        for val in arr: # Iterating over values, not indices\n            if val == min1 and first is None:\n                first = val\n            elif val == min2 and second is None: # Use elif\n                second = val\n            if first is not None and second is not None:\n                break\n        return (first, second)"}
